<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- THE ARMING OF scripts/revision-probe.sh (mg-a03d).
         mg-ce10 landed the external redeploy witness and wired it to nothing:
         501 lines, referenced by a changelog fragment, a docs section and
         test.sh, and by no schedule, no plist and no runner. That is the
         limiting case of the rule the probe itself implements — a detector for
         "X did not happen" must not be ACTIVATED BY X — because a detector
         activated by NOTHING is present by existence and absent by effect.

         WHY LAUNCHD AND NOT THE OTHER TWO CANDIDATES.

         `pogo schedule` was the obvious answer and is the wrong one. Its
         scheduler lives inside pogod and its only delivery mechanisms are a
         nudge or a mail to an AGENT — it cannot run a command. So arming the
         probe that way needs (a) pogod alive and (b) an agent turn to actually
         execute the instruction. Both are failure modes in this exact lineage:
         a stopped pogod is the state the probe most needs to report (mg-6d2f),
         and "turns that never run" is why revision-probe.sh mails itself
         instead of leaving "then mail human" in a scheduler message.

         The deploy runner was the other candidate and is forbidden by the rule:
         a probe invoked by the deploy cannot witness the deploy that never
         fired, which is four of the eight failing nights (mg-2def). That is
         driftwatch's shape (mg-5bd2), and reproducing it here would ship the
         anti-pattern as the fix for itself.

         launchd is triggered by the OS clock. It is independent of pogod, of
         the deploy, of the refinery and of any agent turn. -->
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.pogo.revisionprobe</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <!-- Update YOUR_USERNAME, or use scripts/install-revision-probe.sh.

             The program is the TRACKED script in a checkout, never a copy taken
             at install time. A copy would freeze at the revision that was
             current when somebody last remembered to re-install, which is the
             arming gap one layer down.

             deploy-src rather than ~/dev/pogo: the latter is a working tree a
             human edits and can be mid-rebase, on a feature branch, or dirty at
             any hour. deploy-src is advanced by the deploy runner's sync_src.
             Note what that does and does not mean — a deploy that stops firing
             freezes this script's BODY at the last sync, but it does not stop
             this job FIRING. The witness stays armed; only its text ages. -->
        <string>/Users/YOUR_USERNAME/.pogo/deploy-src/scripts/revision-probe.sh</string>
        <string>--repo</string>
        <string>/Users/YOUR_USERNAME/.pogo/deploy-src</string>
        <string>--url</string>
        <string>http://127.0.0.1:10000</string>
        <string>--stale-after</string>
        <string>24h</string>
        <!-- The stamp is the divergence clock and the re-notify record. Named
             explicitly rather than left to $HOME resolution: an unattended job
             that silently picks a different stamp path than the hand-run does
             has a clock that resets every time an operator checks on it. -->
        <string>--stamp</string>
        <string>/Users/YOUR_USERNAME/.pogo/revision-probe.stamp</string>
        <!-- IT REPORTS EITHER WAY. One ledger line per run, green or red, so
             that "no alert" and "no probe" stop being the same observation.
             The newest line's age is the only thing on this box that can
             answer "is the witness itself still firing?" -->
        <string>--log</string>
        <string>/Users/YOUR_USERNAME/Library/Logs/pogo/revision-probe.log</string>
        <!-- The quiet flag below trims the NARRATIVE, never the ledger and
             never an alert. Without it, the within-threshold report prints
             thirty lines every hour on every day main is ahead of the daemon —
             which is most days — and the alert drowns in the reassurance. The
             ALERT headline is a bare echo and survives it; setup failures go to
             stderr and survive it too.
             (XML comments may not contain a double hyphen, which is why the
             flags are named in prose here rather than quoted literally.) -->
        <string>--quiet</string>
        <!-- The probe mails `human` itself. An alert routed through "and then
             mail someone" in a scheduler message only arrives if a turn runs. -->
        <string>--mail</string>
    </array>

    <!-- HOURLY, at :20, and the two numbers below are chosen together.

         The divergence clock can only mature as fast as the probe samples: a
         once-a-day probe first SEES a divergence up to a day after it starts,
         so a 24h threshold would need three consecutive failed nights to fire.
         Hourly sampling makes a 24h threshold mean 24 hours.

         The cost of sampling hourly is notification volume, and that is paid in
         revision-probe.sh's renotify flag (default 12h) rather than here, because
         the sampling rate and the notification rate answer different questions
         and one schedule field cannot serve both.

         :20 keeps it clear of the 03:00/04:00/05:00 deploy fires and of the
         restart window they open. The probe tolerates that window anyway (three
         tries, two seconds apart) — this is belt and braces, not the guard.

         REPLAY POLICY — DECLARED, because launchd has no field for it and an
         inherited policy is one nobody chose (mg-a03d's own requirement).
         StartCalendarInterval is DEFERRED-ONCE across sleep: a fire missed
         because the host was asleep is delivered once on wake, and ten missed
         hourly fires coalesce into one run, not ten. That is the right policy
         here and the choice is not arbitrary — this probe's report is not a
         per-interval sample. The age it reports is read from a persisted stamp
         against wall clock, so a report delivered late is still TRUE and still
         names the correct age. A skip policy would discard the first report
         after a wake, which is the report most likely to carry news; a count
         policy would replay ten identical reports of one fact.

         WHAT THIS POLICY STILL CANNOT COVER, stated so nobody has to rediscover
         it: a host that is POWERED OFF misses the fire outright — launchd
         defers across sleep, not across shutdown. The 2026-08-07 no-fire nights
         were a power-off, so this witness would have been silent on them too.
         The ledger is how that silence is visible after the fact. -->
    <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>Minute</key>
            <integer>20</integer>
        </dict>
    </array>

    <!-- RunAtLoad=true, and it differs from com.pogo.deploy on purpose. That
         job sets false because a fire bounces the entire fleet, so installing
         it must not be an event. A fire of THIS job is two reads and no
         mutation: it never builds, installs, restarts or writes anything but
         its own stamp and ledger. And the first thing anyone wants after arming
         a witness is evidence that it fires, which this provides at install
         time instead of up to an hour later. -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- One-shot per fire. KeepAlive=true would relaunch it in a loop, since it
         exits on every run by design. -->
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <false/>

    <key>ProcessType</key>
    <string>Background</string>

    <!-- Two files, deliberately. revision-probe.log is the LEDGER — one line
         per run, written by the probe from an EXIT trap, and it is the
         heartbeat. This one catches whatever the run said out loud, which
         under the quiet flag is alerts and setup failures only. Mixing them would
         leave the heartbeat's newest line buried under narrative. -->
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/YOUR_USERNAME/Library/Logs/pogo/revision-probe.report.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/YOUR_USERNAME/Library/Logs/pogo/revision-probe.report.log</string>

    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <!-- Must resolve git, curl and mg. It deliberately does NOT need to
             resolve `go`, `pogo` or `pogod`: the probe must run on a box where
             the deploy has been failing for a week, and reaching for anything
             the deploy installs would arm it only by a deploy that worked.
             ~/go/bin is on the path for `mg` alone — scripts/revision-probe.sh
             makes every candidate self-identify as macguffin before trusting
             it, because /usr/bin/mg is the Micro-Emacs editor. -->
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/Users/YOUR_USERNAME/go/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
        <key>HOME</key>
        <string>/Users/YOUR_USERNAME</string>
        <!-- NO GH_TOKEN HERE, EVER. ~/Library/LaunchAgents is world-readable.
             The probe reads a public ref over https and needs no credential;
             GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 keeps a repo that suddenly wants one from
             HANGING, which is how an unattended probe goes silent without
             failing. -->
        <key>GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT</key>
        <string>0</string>
    </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
